David Damerell wrote:
> [Object ID spoiler update]

First and foremost, thank you again for this wonderful spoiler. And
thanks for the diff between the two versions, it helps guys like who
would have otherwise reread the entire thing to find the changes.

I don't know if you list this technique or not (I don't have time
to read through the spoiler right now), so I'll go through it in
full. Say you buy a potion of water from a shop. Since it costed only
6z, you know it is uncursed. Do not BUC-test the potion; instead #name
the stack 'uncursed'. Now any water that stacks with this stack is
known to be uncursed, the name will propagate. Any water that does
not stack is either blessed or cursed. You could use a pet to
determine which (as pets will only reluctantly step on cursed items).
If you have multiple potions in a stack that are known to be blessed
or cursed, you could split the stack into two and identify one of the
new stacks (preferably with an altar); this won't affect the other
stack. Naturally this works for any stackable item type, but is most
easily abused for water (because it's easier to identify the BUC
status of water than for most other item types).

This is a neat technique with a lot of possibilities and
applications. I have quite a few more words to say about it. I'll
write a proper, more thorough explanation of it tomorrow. An entire
book could be written about discovering the BUC status of a stack
without an altar (gogo atheism!).

And now, some fixes and improvements for you.

> If you have an unidentified touchstone or any other grey stone,
> rubbing it on metal armour produces "scritch, scritch" or "scratch
> marks", and non-metallic armour produces brown streaks. You can't
> do this with an identified touchstone. No, I don't know why not.

This is a genuine bug (C343-130) that'll be fixed in a future release.
The DevTeam apparently hadn't anticipated using a touchstone to
identify metallic objects, so when you apply a known touchstone, it
lists only gems.

> A cloak of magic resistance could also be identified by walking
> into traps of types that it negates[...]

This is also a viable strategy for certain rings. Most notably, it
provides an easy way to identify a ring of teleport control. You
could also use it for polymorph traps, but that sounds a bit too
risky. Certainly worth mentioning, in my opinion.

> If you find a bag in a bones pile, it is mostly likely cursed and
> full of juicy loot. You may not want to chance its being holding
> (and losing some of the loot), so if you can lift it at all, it's
> probably a good idea to uncurse it first; if not, clear other
> objects away and give it a zap of cancellation (if possible) -
> this doesn't harm the contents.

I'd mention that this doesn't harm the bag, either. For some reason
people think cancelling a bag of holding will turn it into an
ordinary sack.

I'm having trouble phrasing this next bit succinctly, so bear with
me. :) Putting a charged wand of cancellation into a bag of holding
(usually) makes the bag explode. This means that any /oCanc found in
the bones pile would very likely not be in the bag. This is handy
because you can use the deceased player's wand to uncurse his bag.

> It's not clear to me that cursed holding can be generated outside
> of a bones pile.

The only time you would encounter a new cursed bag of holding is on
a bones level. It doesn't necessarily have to be in the pile. Bags
of holding cannot be generated cursed.


